\description{
Tests, within each \verb{(rank, feature, group_col)} stratum, whether peptide
prevalence differs between groups of subjects (\strong{group} term), changes
over timepoints within subjects (\strong{time} term), or changes differently
over time between groups (\strong{interaction}). Unlike \code{\link[=compute_delta]{compute_delta()}},
every sample is its own row: a subject's samples are never collapsed, so
the number of samples per subject does not act as a hidden covariate.
}
\details{
\strong{Per-peptide statistic.} Each term is a score statistic standardised by
its exact permutation moments:
\itemize{
\item \emph{group}: each subject contributes its mean positivity minus the
weighted mean over subjects, times its weight
\eqn{w_i = n_i / (1 + (n_i - 1)\rho)} (\code{rho} below); group labels are
resampled across subjects with the group sizes fixed. Centring at the
weighted mean keeps unequal sample counts from shifting every peptide
together. With one sample
per subject and two groups this is \code{compute_delta(stat_mode = "score")}
up to the constant factor \eqn{\sqrt{(N-1)/N}}.
\item \emph{time}: each subject's deviations from its own mean (the fixed-effects
"within" estimator), with timepoints reshuffled inside each subject. With
two complete timepoints this is the McNemar z; with \code{T} it is Cochran's Q.
Subjects with one sample contribute nothing.
\item \emph{interaction}: the within-subject deviations, residualised against the
pooled time main effect, compared across group labels and standardised
by a Welch-type variance (one residual variance per group, shaped by
each subject's visits, and accounting for the estimated time effect),
recomputed for every draw. The interaction's null is about
mean changes, so this keeps it calibrated when groups differ in
turnover or visit patterns. A timepoint with no samples in some group
leaves the interaction there not estimable; the test stays valid but is
conservative, and a warning names the gap. Known limitation: when
groups miss visits at random and positivity also changes over time, the
interaction can run mildly liberal (about 0.066 at a nominal 0.05 in
the package validation, \code{inst/validation}).
}

A term with one degree of freedom (two levels, or \code{group_scores} /
\code{time_scores} for a linear trend) gives a signed z per peptide; an omnibus
term (more levels) gives a vector whose squared length is the term's
quadratic form.

\strong{Pooling.} Per-peptide values are winsorised at \code{winsor_z} and pooled per
component as in \code{\link[=compute_delta]{compute_delta()}} (\code{weight_mode}, \code{aggregate_stat},
strata of pooled prevalence from \code{strat_bins}) into the profile \code{E}. The
term statistic is \code{T = E} for one degree of freedom (signed, like
\code{\link[=compute_delta]{compute_delta()}}'s \code{T_obs}) and \code{T = sum(E^2)} otherwise; p-values compare
\verb{|T|} with its permutation null. \code{n_eff_sqrt} weights by the square root
of a peptide's positive samples in the contrast; \code{se_invvar} by
\eqn{1/\sqrt{p_0(1 - p_0)}} of its pooled prevalence.

\strong{Weights choose the effect scale.} A peptide's z is roughly its
prevalence difference divided by \eqn{\sqrt{p(1 - p)}}, so the weights
decide which common effect the pooled statistic is most powerful for:
\code{equal} for a common shift on the arcsine-square-root scale, \code{n_eff_sqrt}
(the default) for a common log odds ratio (\eqn{\sqrt{n p} \approx
\sqrt{n p (1 - p)}} for rare peptides), and \code{se_invvar} for a common
prevalence difference. Within a \code{strat_bins} stratum prevalences are
alike, so the weights matter mostly across strata; with the default strata
every non-empty stratum counts equally, and \code{strat_bins = 0} lets the
weights act across the whole prevalence range. See
\code{vignette("delta2-analysis")}.

\strong{Standardisation.} The per-peptide z of the group term has mean 0 and
SD 1 under uniform relabelling, but not exactly under propensity draws,
the swap chain or the within-subject coins, and a peptide whose null is
off-centre moves the pooled statistic in every draw. With
\code{standardize = "two_pass"} a first pass over the draws records each
peptide's mean and SD of every component (over the observed labels and
the \code{B_permutations} draws, so the observed labelling stays exchangeable
with the draws), and a second pass replays the same draws and pools the
standardised values (winsorised after standardising). This holds for any
sampler, at twice the cost and \eqn{O(m)} extra memory. Under uniform
relabelling it changes the group term little; the gain is with a
propensity model.

\strong{Resampling.} The group term, the interaction and the pairwise tests
share one set of \code{B_permutations} subject relabellings per \code{group_col}:
a uniform split of the subjects with the observed group sizes, or, with
\code{propensity_formula}, draws from the conditional law of the labels given
the covariates (rejection sampling for two groups, the pairwise-swap
chain of Berrett et al. 2020 for more). With \code{propensity_formula} the
group term and the pairwise tests are also adjusted for its covariates:
the subject means are residualised on them by weighted least squares
before the groups are compared (the score test for group in a model with
the covariates). The draws make the p-value valid; the adjustment keeps
the statistic from being dominated by the covariates, without which the
test is conservative and loses its power. The time term uses within-subject
relabellings: a coin per two-sample subject (biased by
\code{time_propensity_formula}) or a uniform permutation of the subject's
timepoints. The draws depend only on seeds taken from R's RNG, so a given
\code{set.seed()} gives the same results on every platform.

\strong{Type inference} (\code{"auto"}): two levels are binary; more levels of a
factor or character column are multi; a numeric column is multi with at
most 10 distinct values and otherwise an error, since continuous group and
time columns are not supported yet. A \code{group_col} must be constant within
subject; a column that varies within subject belongs in \code{time_col}.

\strong{Pairwise tests and max-T.} With \code{pairwise = TRUE} every pair of group
levels gets the binary group statistic, computed from the same subject
relabellings. \code{p_perm} is the pair's own permutation p-value; \code{p_maxT}
compares the pair's \verb{|T|} with the maximum over all pairs in each draw,
which controls the family-wise error over pairs. Its null is "no group
differs": a claim about one pair while another group truly differs needs
that pair's own null.

\strong{Effect sizes.} \verb{max_delta_*} is the largest absolute per-peptide effect:
the difference in subject-mean prevalence between the top and bottom
(scored) group for the group term, the within-subject change between the
extreme timepoints for the time term, and the difference of those changes
between groups for the interaction; for omnibus terms, the largest range
across levels (and the \verb{frac_delta_pos_*} columns are \code{NA}). The \verb{_adj}
version weights subjects by their inverse propensity (Hajek-normalised
within group), so it is adjusted for the covariates in
\code{propensity_formula}; without a propensity model it equals the raw one.
The time term's effect is a within-subject estimate and is not
re-weighted.
}
